  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "In \\(\\triangle ABC\\) and \\(\\triangle DEF\\), \\(\\angle A=\\angle D\\), \\(\\angle B=\\angle E\\), and \\(BC=EF\\). Which congruence criterion can be used?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "ASA",
    "B": "AAS",
    "C": "SAS",
    "D": "SSS"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "\\(BC\\) is opposite \\(\\angle A\\), and \\(EF\\) is opposite \\(\\angle D\\). Two angles and the side opposite one of them are respectively equal, so AAS directly proves \\(\\triangle ABC\\cong\\triangle DEF\\), giving choice B.\n\nThe wording also permits finding the third angles. By the triangle angle sum,\n\\[\n\\angle C=180^\\circ-\\angle A-\\angle B\n=180^\\circ-\\angle D-\\angle E=\\angle F.\n\\]\nNow \\(BC\\) and \\(EF\\) are the included sides between the equal angle pairs at \\(B,C\\) and \\(E,F\\). Thus ASA also proves congruence, giving choice A. Both A and B can be used; B is the intended answer only under a direct-given-pattern interpretation.",
